      "text": "To solve the inequality \\(\\frac{x + 6}{x^2 + 2x + 7} \\ge 0\\), we need to analyze the sign of the expression \\(\\frac{x + 6}{x^2 + 2x + 7}\\).\n\n### Step 1: Determine the Sign of the Numerator\nThe numerator is \\(x + 6\\). This expression is zero when \\(x = -6\\) and positive when \\(x > -6\\) and negative when \\(x < -6\\).\n\n### Step 2: Determine the Sign of the Denominator\nThe denominator is \\(x^2 + 2x + 7\\). To analyze this, we first check if the denominator has real roots. The discriminant \\(\\Delta\\) of the quadratic equation \\(x^2 + 2x + 7 = 0\\) is given by:\n\\[\n\\Delta = b^2 - 4ac = 2^2 - 4 \\cdot 1 \\cdot 7 = 4 - 28 = -24\n\\]\nSince the discriminant is negative, the quadratic equation \\(x^2 + 2x + 7 = 0\\) has no real roots. This means the quadratic \\(x^2 + 2x + 7\\) is always positive for all real \\(x\\).\n\n### Step 3: Analyze the Inequality\nSince the denominator \\(x^2 + 2x + 7\\) is always positive, the sign of the fraction \\(\\frac{x + 6}{x^2 + 2x + 7}\\) depends solely on the sign of the numerator \\(x + 6\\).\n\n- The fraction \\(\\frac{x + 6}{x^2 + 2x + 7} \\ge 0\\) when \\(x + 6 \\ge 0\\).\n- This inequality \\(x + 6 \\ge 0\\) simplifies to \\(x \\ge -6\\).\n\n### Step 4: Consider the Domain of the Original Expression\nThe original expression \\(\\frac{x + 6}{x^2 + 2x + 7}\\) is undefined when the denominator is zero. As we have seen, the denominator \\(x^2 + 2x + 7\\) is never zero for any real \\(x\\). Therefore, the only restriction is from the numerator.\n\n### Step 5: Combine the Results\nThe inequality \\(\\frac{x + 6}{x^2 + 2x + 7} \\ge 0\\) holds when \\(x \\ge -6\\). Since the denominator is always positive, the expression is defined for all real numbers \\(x \\ge -6\\).\n\nThus, the solution to the inequality is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{[-6, \\infty)}\n\\]",
