      "text": "To solve the inequality \\(\\frac{x + 6}{x^2 + 2x + 7} \\ge 0\\), we need to determine the values of \\(x\\) for which the fraction is non-negative. This involves analyzing the sign of the numerator and the denominator separately.\n\nFirst, let's consider the denominator \\(x^2 + 2x + 7\\).\nThe quadratic expression \\(x^2 + 2x + 7\\) can be rewritten by completing the square:\n\\[x^2 + 2x + 7 = (x+1)^2 + 6.\\]\nSince \\((x+1)^2 \\ge 0\\) for all real \\(x\\), it follows that \\((x+1)^2 + 6 \\ge 6\\), which means \\(x^2 + 2x + 7 > 0\\) for all real \\(x\\). Therefore, the denominator is always positive and never zero, so it does not affect the sign of the fraction.\n\nNext, we analyze the numerator \\(x + 6\\). The numerator is zero when \\(x = -6\\), and it changes sign at this point. We also need to determine where the numerator is positive or negative.\n\nThe numerator \\(x + 6\\) is positive when \\(x > -6\\) and negative when \\(x < -6\\).\n\nNow, we combine these findings:\n- The fraction \\(\\frac{x + 6}{x^2 + 2x + 7}\\) is positive when both the numerator and the denominator are positive, or when the numerator is negative and the denominator is positive. Since the denominator is always positive, the fraction is positive when the numerator is positive.\n- The numerator is positive when \\(x > -6\\). Additionally, since the denominator is positive for all \\(x\\), the fraction is positive when the numerator is positive, which is always true for \\(x > -6\\).\n\nTherefore, the inequality \\(\\frac{x + 6}{x^2 + 2x + 7} \\ge 0\\) is satisfied when \\(x \\ge -6\\).\n\nIn interval notation, the solution is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{[-6, \\infty)}\n\\]",
